<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>hr.hrg.jcodebuddy</groupId>
        <artifactId>jcodebuddy-parent</artifactId>
        <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>jcodebuddy-core</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>JCodeBuddy Core</name>
    <description>
        The metadata engine: the one source representation and the parse path over it, the metadata model,
        the class index with its content identity, and the queries a consumer asks of them — one engine for
        codegen, analysis, reporting and the watch loop (DEC-037). It also carries the generated-code marker
        vocabulary and its parser (DEC-035).
    </description>

    <!--
        WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS, AND WHAT IT CARRIES NOW.

        Generated code is committed source and it is cooperative: a member from an earlier revision
        survives regeneration, so a file can be partly generated and partly hand-written, and the boundary
        used to be knowable only to the generator — because the recogniser WAS the generator. Every other
        consumer (an external static parser, an AI agent reading the source, a reviewer reading a diff)
        needs that boundary and has no access to a generator's internals. DEC-035 fixes the marker
        vocabulary; this module is the parser that reads it.

        Since 2026-10-02 (DEC-037, plan step 3.0f) this module is also the home of the metadata engine, and
        that is why it now declares OpenRewrite and Jackson: parsing goes through DEC-030's one
        representation, and the metadata documents are JSON. The engine is here because a consumer that
        needs fresh metadata — a generator, an analysis, a report, an LSP sidecar — must read ONE model
        rather than each growing a path of its own.

        This module is therefore no longer a leaf, and the reason it used to be one has not gone away: a
        tool that only wants to know where generated code stops should not resolve a parser. Step 3.0l
        extracts the marker vocabulary and its parser into a small leaf of their own, which is what restores
        that property; until it lands, this module carries both.

        The direction of the dependency is still the honest one, and step 3.0f-2 was spent proving it: the
        engine names no consumer. Where it needed what a consumer had (a kind resolver, a diagnostic
        channel, a member shape, a JSON helper, the `.jcodebuddy` name), the engine defines the port or owns
        the vocabulary, and the consumer implements or delegates.
    -->

    <dependencies>
        <!-- The one source representation (DEC-030): read, query and write Java through the LST, never a
             second parser. `rewrite-java-25` is the JDK 25 dialect the gate compiles at. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-java</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.openrewrite</groupId>
            <artifactId>rewrite-java-25</artifactId>
            <version>${openrewrite.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!-- The metadata documents are JSON (`.jcodebuddy/index/classes.json`, DEC-029). Jackson 3 is what
             this reactor manages (`tools.jackson.core`, `${jackson.version}`), not the `com.fasterxml` line. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>tools.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Test-only, and the parser itself stayed pure JDK where it could. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>
</project>
